# THE TWO HALVES OF EVERY CLAIM'S UNCERTAINTY, READ TOGETHER. One row per combination of what
# would make a range smaller and who owns the edge it would have to move, counted over the
# corpus.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS THE PAIR THE SCHEMA WAS RESTRUCTURED TO MAKE ASKABLE, AND FOR TWO REVISIONS
#    NOTHING ASKED IT. `narrowsWhen` and `boundOrigin` were both promoted to required and
#    typed on `Claim` precisely because they are one instrument -- and they were ingested into
#    two tables joined by nothing, so the repository printed two censuses and never the
#    crossing. The ingest comment even said the ordinal existed "so the two can be joined";
#    no query ever did.
#
# ⛔⛔⛔ READ THE LARGEST CELL FIRST AND IT IS THE ONE THAT SHOULD SMART. "Nobody has said what
#    would narrow this" crossed with "nothing sets this bound" is a claim with NO COURSE OF
#    ACTION AND NO OWNER -- a bare number pair wearing a three-point estimate's clothes.
#    Neither census alone can see it: in one it is an unremarkable `unmeasured`, in the other
#    an unremarkable `none`. It is the honest measure of how much of this corpus is decoration.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE SMALL CELLS ARE WHERE THE ADVICE IS. `instrument` against `contractual` says
#    measure it and then go renegotiate; `intervention` against `intrinsic` says the bound
#    does not move at all, so the only way out is to stop being bound by that thing -- which
#    is exactly what the one row filing it proposes, in prose, in this corpus.
#
# ⛔ CORPUS ONLY. The fixtures file combinations on purpose to prove the branches work, and
#   counting a stipulation as evidence of what people actually write is the error this whole
#   scope/ directory exists to prevent.
-- epistemics/widths.sqlc against epistemics/edges.sqlc, joined on the claim they share.
SELECT w.what_the_width_is_made_of AS what_would_narrow_it,
       e.who_owns_the_edge         AS who_owns_the_edge,
       count(*)                    AS claims
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/widths.sqlc)
) w
JOIN      (
    :compose(epistemics/edges.sqlc)
) e USING (filing, seq)
JOIN      (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY 3 DESC, 1, 2
